 1 big thing: Early voting surges in key states 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 People hold signs directing voters to a polling place Wednesday in Minneapolis. Photo: Stephen Maturen/Getty Images This year's early voting is significantly outpacing the last midterm elections, according to an Associated Press analysis of voting data five days before Election Day.

 More than 43 million Americans have voted early in person or returned mail-in ballots — nearly 10 million more than in 2022. The figures cover 47 states and D.C., compared with 48 states and D.C. four years ago. 🏛️ There have been particularly sharp increases in states hosting big competitive races.

 In Texas, where voters are deciding closely watched Senate, House and governor's races, more than 6.3 million people voted early as of Wednesday. That's compared with 4.2 million at the same point four years ago, a 50% increase. Candidates' signs outside a polling place in Houston on Oct. 19. Photo: Ronaldo Schemidt/AFP via Getty Images 🗳️ Early voting has also increased more than 30% from the same point four years ago in Maine, Michigan, North Carolina and Kansas.

 In South Carolina, where voters are choosing a successor to the late Republican Sen. Lindsey Graham, early voting is up 83%. Between the lines: In states that provide party affiliation data, about 6.8 million Democrats and 5.2 million Republicans have voted early.

 Those figures don't account for all early voters nationwide, or indicate which candidates received their votes. Go deeper . 

 
 
