 4. ⏰ 1 for the road: Hacking the time change 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios A few small tweaks can ease this weekend's "fall back" to standard time, Renske Lok, a circadian biologist at the University of Colorado Boulder, tells Axios Denver's Alayna Alvarez. 

 Here's how to hack it: 

 🛌 Start tonight. Shift your bedtime and wake-up time back by 10–12 minutes a day to give your internal clock a head start. 👶 Parents, beware. Babies and kids don't understand the shift to standard time, so a 6 a.m. wake-up call could become 5 a.m. 💤 Don't count on bonus sleep. Data from Oura users shows that they slept less overall the week after the 2024 fall time change, despite gaining an hour. ☀️ Chase the morning sun. Getting outside early helps your body adjust. Go deeper ... Get Axios Local . 

 
 
